{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And I will take of the spirit, etc.</b> The reason for this sequence is to show him that HaShem had placed in him the power to bear the burden of all the people, and when he declined, He took back from him what the elders who would come needed to bear the burden of the people together with him." "<b>And you shall not bear alone.</b> Here He finalized the withdrawal from him — meaning that even if Moses were to reconsider and say, ״I will bear it alone,״ HaShem said to him: you shall not bear alone. Further, He hinted to him that through the very implementation of the matter, the leadership and the preeminence would no longer remain for Moses alone, and this is the expression <b>and you shall not bear</b> (ve-lo tissa) — in the sense of the preeminence of a prince (nesi'ut): you shall not be alone as at first. Then there came to hand the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 15), and this is their language: The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: I gave you understanding and knowledge to sustain My children, and you were distinguished by that greatness — now they are taking from what is yours. End quote. It further intends to be explained according to the reasoning of those who say (Sanhedrin 17a) ״and they did not continue״ — meaning they did not cease, and accordingly <b>and you shall not bear</b> (ve-lo tissa) is in the language of prophecy, as it says (Malachi 1:1) ״מַשָּׂא דְבַר ה׳״ — meaning, you shall not be a prophet alone, but all the elders shall also be prophets."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:40.524789636Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}